CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute exacerbation of somatic and psychological distress directly linked to accumulated social stressors, manifesting as severe myofascial pain, resting tachycardia (HR 98), and a 48-hour cessation of sleep. This acute sympathetic overdrive and functional collapse required immediate, out-of-hours, face-to-face assessment to rule out primary acute organic pathology and to provide immediate pharmacological and non-pharmacological stabilization. Delaying assessment until the next available in-hours appointment posed an unacceptable risk of further physiological decompensation, severe exhaustion, and potential harm due to cognitive depletion, strictly satisfying the criteria for an urgent after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered single dose of diazepam 2mg orally to break the acute muscle spasm and autonomic hyperarousal cycle. Recommended routine fasting morning cortisol, systemic inflammatory markers, and full blood count via usual GP.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce somatic tension, practice progressive muscle relaxation for 10 minutes twice daily; rewarding this completion with a preferred low-stress activity will reinforce the habit. To manage cognitive depletion, implement a strict limit on social media and advocacy engagement to 30 minutes daily, positively reinforcing the boundary by dedicating the saved time to restorative rest.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to community psychology services for targeted cognitive behavioral therapy.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated at this time.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 72 hours for ongoing management and review of expected investigation results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if experiencing chest pain, palpitations lasting longer than 15 minutes, acute shortness of breath, or sudden emergence of active suicidal ideation with intent.

